<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="husky" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
<!--
  husky_real_zedx.urdf.xacro - the real robot plus the ZED X camera. Just
  these two - the bracket/holder visual geometry was removed (it never
  affected anything functional: the camera's position and rigidity come
  entirely from the fixed joint below, not from what mesh is drawn there).

  zedx_parent/zedx_xyz/zedx_rpy is what to edit to move the camera - see
  ENGINEERING_NOTES.md for the guided walkthrough of editing this in VS Code.

  husky_real.urdf is a flat, already-expanded URDF (not itself a xacro file).
  xacro:include still works on it: xacro pulls in the CHILDREN of its
  <robot> root (every link/joint/gazebo block) and discards that file's own
  <robot> wrapper, so there is exactly one <robot> root here, not two.
-->

  <xacro:include filename="$(find sidewalk_sim)/urdf/husky_real.urdf"/>
  <xacro:include filename="$(find sidewalk_bringup)/urdf/zedx_mount.urdf.xacro"/>

  <!-- zedx_base_frame is the ZED X's own dedicated mount point, built into
       husky_real.urdf's "ZED X Camera Integration" section - the same way
       the sensor arch already has its own oak-d-base-frame for a different,
       earlier camera. It isn't shared with anything else, so zedx_xyz here
       is normally "0 0 0": the position IS the frame, tuned once and baked
       in, rather than an offset re-applied on every launch. Change the
       frame's own position in husky_real.urdf if the mount ever needs to
       move for real; use zedx_xyz here only for a temporary, try-it-and-see
       nudge. D05 (a PACS cell on the main deck) also still works as
       zedx_parent, for an unrelated, lower mount.

       WHAT THESE THREE MOVE, PRECISELY: the camera's MOUNT POINT - the
       outside of the housing where the bracket grips it - and everything
       inside the camera rides along with it. They are NOT the lens pose.
       See the block below for why that distinction has already cost this
       project a run's worth of data. -->
  <xacro:arg name="zedx_parent" default="zedx_base_frame"/>
  <xacro:arg name="zedx_xyz" default="0 0 0"/>
  <xacro:arg name="zedx_rpy" default="0 0 0"/>

  <!-- stereo camera mode + its image noise: see zedx_mount.urdf.xacro for
       what these do and why. Defaults preserve the existing depth camera. -->
  <xacro:arg name="zedx_stereo" default="false"/>
  <xacro:arg name="zedx_noise_stddev" default="0.005"/>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THE CAMERA'S OWN INSIDE: mount -> camera_center -> LEFT LENS.

       WHY THIS CHAIN EXISTS AT ALL, AND WHY DELETING IT BREAKS SIM-VS-REAL.

       A ZED X reports depth from its LEFT LENS. The left lens is not where
       the bracket holds the camera: it sits 60 mm to the robot's LEFT and
       16 mm ABOVE the mount point, and 10 mm back along x. Those three
       numbers are the camera's internal geometry - they are properties of
       the product, not of this robot, and they are the same on every ZED X.

       UNTIL 2026-08-29 THIS FILE DID NOT HAVE THEM. It handed the mount
       pose straight to the zedx_camera macro, and that macro's contract -
       stated in its own header, in the paragraph that begins "Fixes a real
       bug" - is that the pose it is given IS THE LEFT LENS POSE. So the
       simulated left lens was pinned exactly on the mount point: 60 mm to
       the RIGHT of, and 16 mm BELOW, where the real one is. That is
       precisely the 60 mm error the macro was written to prevent,
       reintroduced one level up by its only caller.

       WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN 60 mm SOUNDS. The entire justification for
       the simulation is that a sim run and a real run can be compared -
       same route, same SLAM settings, one variable changed at a time
       (ENGINEERING_NOTES.md section 4, rule 5). A fixed 60 mm lateral offset between
       the two cameras is not noise that averages out over a run; it is a
       constant bias baked into every observation, on one side of the
       comparison only. It would have shown up as a small, stubborn,
       unexplainable sim-vs-real disagreement - the most expensive kind,
       because there is nothing in a trajectory plot that says "your camera
       is in the wrong place".

       THE SHAPE OF THE TREE, NOT JUST THE ENDPOINT. It would have been one
       line shorter to bolt the whole (-0.010, +0.060, +0.016) onto zedx_xyz
       and be done. This file builds the two intermediate links instead, so
       that the simulated transform tree has the SAME SHAPE as the one the
       real ZED driver publishes:

           zedx_base_frame            robot URDF's mount point on the arch
             -> zedx_front_base_link      the driver's own root
               -> zedx_front_camera_center    middle of the housing
                 -> zedx_front_left_camera_frame       <- the left lens
                 -> zedx_front_right_camera_frame
                 -> ..._optical_frame for each

       Three things follow from that, and none of them follow from the
       one-line version:
         * The frame NAMES match, so anything that names a ZED frame - an
           RViz config, a tf_echo in a procedure, a bag audit - works
           unchanged against sim and hardware.
         * The one verification command the real robot uses,
               rosrun tf tf_echo zedx_base_frame zedx_front_base_link
           now also runs in simulation, and must read [0,0,0] in BOTH
           translation and rotation there too (with zedx_xyz/zedx_rpy at
           their defaults). One check, both worlds.
         * A future correction to the camera's internals lands in one
           obvious place instead of being smeared into a mount offset that
           a "temporary nudge" would silently wipe out.

       PROVENANCE OF THE THREE NUMBERS. Read from the LIVE transform tree
       on the Jetson with the camera running, 2026-08-29, and recorded in
       three places that already agree with each other:
           sidewalk_bringup/config/robot_frames.yaml       camera_front
           sidewalk_bringup/urdf/sidewalk_robot.urdf.xacro the FRONT CAMERA
                                                           POSE block
           sidewalk_perception/launch/zedx_front.launch    PHYSICAL MOUNTING
       The base->centre part (+0.016 in z) is also half the 31.8 mm body
       height from the Stereolabs ZED X datasheet Rev 1.6, which is an
       independent way of arriving at the same figure.

       [UNVERIFIED FROM THIS MACHINE] The split of the total into
       base->centre and centre->lens is taken from the driver's own macro,
           ~/catkin_ws/src/zed-ros-wrapper/zed_wrapper/urdf/zed_macro.urdf.xacro
       on the JETSON. That file was not present where this was written, so the split
       was not re-read here. The SUM is what every measurement above
       constrains, and the sum is what the verification checks; if the split
       is ever found to be different, only camera_center moves, and the lens
       - the point that matters - does not.

       DO NOT put these numbers in zedx_xyz, and do not copy
       robot_frames.yaml's camera_front x/y/z into the mount joint in
       husky_real.urdf. Both of those are the same mistake wearing a
       different hat, and both are called out by name in the files above.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_base_to_centre_z" value="0.016"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_centre_to_left_x" value="-0.010"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_centre_to_left_y" value="0.060"/>

  <!-- The driver's root. Coincident with the robot URDF's mount frame by
       construction, exactly as it is on the real robot - that is the whole
       content of the tf_echo check quoted above. zedx_xyz/zedx_rpy ride on
       THIS joint, so a nudge moves the mount and the camera's insides
       follow, rather than a nudge quietly cancelling the internals. -->
  <link name="zedx_front_base_link"/>
  <joint name="zedx_front_base_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="$(arg zedx_parent)"/>
    <child link="zedx_front_base_link"/>
    <origin xyz="$(arg zedx_xyz)" rpy="$(arg zedx_rpy)"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Middle of the housing. No rotation: the ZED's internal frames are all
       axis-aligned with its base, and the mount's 3.24 deg nose-down pitch
       is applied ONCE, upstream, on zedx_base_joint in husky_real.urdf.
       Putting a second copy of the pitch here would double it. -->
  <link name="zedx_front_camera_center"/>
  <joint name="zedx_front_camera_center_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="zedx_front_base_link"/>
    <child link="zedx_front_camera_center"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 ${zedx_base_to_centre_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- The macro's xyz IS the left lens, measured from the parent given here
       (camera_center). That is the macro's documented contract and this is
       the call that finally honours it.

       KNOWN RESIDUAL, deliberate and harmless: the macro derives the
       housing link (zedx_front_link - the mesh, the collision box and the
       240 g of inertia) as lens minus half the baseline in y ONLY, so it
       lands 10 mm behind camera_center in x rather than on top of it. The
       macro has no way to know about the lens's 10 mm optical offset. That
       10 mm affects a visual mesh, a collision box nothing touches, and the
       inertia of a quarter-kilo part on a 35 kg robot. It affects NO camera
       frame and NO measurement. Fixing it properly means teaching
       zedx_mount.urdf.xacro about the optical offset, which is a change to
       a file shared with the real robot's model and is deliberately not
       being made from inside the simulation package. -->
  <xacro:zedx_camera name="zedx_front" parent="zedx_front_camera_center"
                      xyz="${zedx_centre_to_left_x} ${zedx_centre_to_left_y} 0"
                      rpy="0 0 0"
                      stereo="$(arg zedx_stereo)"
                      noise_stddev="$(arg zedx_noise_stddev)"/>

</robot>
